<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antibes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Antipolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antibes is a town on the French Riviera between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. It began as Antipolis, a Greek trading settlement founded by Massalia, facing the Ligurian peoples of the hills behind it. When the Deciates and Oxybii besieged it in 154 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> intervened on Massalia's behalf. It later became a Roman town and, in modern times, a fortified French port on the border with Savoyard Nice.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antibes.jpg" alt="Antibes" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilbert Bochenek · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antibesbordmer.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
